WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.678
You have counted pages.

00:00:05.678 --> 00:00:09.356
You have shrunk them with compression.

00:00:09.356 --> 00:00:13.034
Now, how do you arrange the bytes inside a page in the first place?

00:00:13.034 --> 00:00:16.712
That choice is the storage layout, and it is the single biggest performance lever in any database.

00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:20.390
Same data, two layouts, ten times the IO.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.812
Start with the table your query sees.

00:00:23.812 --> 00:00:27.234
Listens, with user id, song id, rating, and listen time.

00:00:27.234 --> 00:00:30.656
That is the logical view.

00:00:30.656 --> 00:00:34.078
On disk you can store it two ways.

00:00:34.078 --> 00:00:37.500
Row-oriented packs whole rows together.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.922
Page one holds three complete listens, page two holds the next two.

00:00:40.922 --> 00:00:44.344
Read listen forty-two and all four columns come back in one read.

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:47.767
An insert is one packed write.

00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:51.189
Columnar flips it.

00:00:51.189 --> 00:00:54.611
Each column gets its own file.

00:00:54.611 --> 00:00:58.033
All the user ids together, then all the ratings, then all the listen times.

00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:01.455
Now AVG of rating across a billion listens touches the rating file and skips the other three.

00:01:01.455 --> 00:01:04.877
On this four-column toy that is seventy-five percent of the IO saved, and far more on a wide table.

00:01:04.877 --> 00:01:08.299
The query decides the winner.

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:11.721
Point lookups and inserts want row.

00:01:11.721 --> 00:01:15.143
Big aggregates want columnar.

00:01:15.143 --> 00:01:19.520
So how does one row-oriented page actually pack variable-length rows?

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.897
With a slotted page.

00:01:23.897 --> 00:01:28.274
Data records grow from the top down.

00:01:28.274 --> 00:01:32.651
Row A, Row B, a longer Row C.

00:01:32.651 --> 00:01:37.028
The slot array grows from the bottom up.

00:01:37.028 --> 00:01:41.405
Each slot is just an offset and a length, a pointer to where its row lives.

00:01:41.405 --> 00:01:45.782
S0 points at Row A, S1 at Row B.

00:01:45.782 --> 00:01:50.159
Free space sits in the middle and shrinks as both sides grow toward each other.

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:54.535
The payoff is updates.

00:01:54.535 --> 00:01:58.912
Change a row in place and it is free, as long as the size holds.

00:01:58.912 --> 00:02:03.289
If the row grows past its spot, it moves elsewhere and the slot repoints.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:07.666
External references like page id and slot id never change.

00:02:07.666 --> 00:02:12.043
Columnar pages flip this, and they earn a second prize.

00:02:12.043 --> 00:02:16.420
A page holds many values of one column, all the same type, which is exactly what encoders feast on.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.797
At a hundred million rows the same column shrinks five to one, even ten to one.

00:02:20.797 --> 00:02:25.174
Repetition only emerges at scale, and that is why BigQuery and Snowflake scan petabytes efficiently.

00:02:25.174 --> 00:02:29.911
Pure columnar has a catch.

00:02:29.911 --> 00:02:34.648
Each column in its own file makes one row a seek per column, and a wide table explodes into a hundred thousand tiny files that object stores like S three punish.

00:02:34.648 --> 00:02:39.385
Parquet fixes it.

00:02:39.385 --> 00:02:44.121
It slices the rows into row groups, each about one hundred twenty eight megabytes, roughly a million rows.

00:02:44.121 --> 00:02:48.858
The file is a sequence of row groups.

00:02:48.858 --> 00:02:53.595
Inside one row group, each column sits together as a column chunk.

00:02:53.595 --> 00:02:58.332
The rating chunk is all the ratings, contiguous, so a scan reads only it, and each chunk compresses on its own, ratings run length, timestamps delta.

00:02:58.332 --> 00:03:03.069
Drill in once more.

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:07.806
A column chunk is a stack of small pages, about one megabyte each.

00:03:07.806 --> 00:03:12.543
That is Parquet's page, not the sixty four megabyte DbFile page.

00:03:12.543 --> 00:03:17.280
Same word, different size.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.017
A footer at the end holds each column's min and max per row group.

00:03:22.017 --> 00:03:26.753
A query reads it first and skips whole row groups that cannot match.

00:03:26.753 --> 00:03:31.490
The trade is simple.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:36.227
You pay a sliver of compression, since a run cannot cross a row group boundary.

00:03:36.227 --> 00:03:40.964
You gain local rows, one file instead of a hundred thousand, and a row group sized for parallel scans.

00:03:40.964 --> 00:03:44.207
The query pattern picks the layout.

00:03:44.207 --> 00:03:47.450
Lookups, updates, and inserts favor row.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.693
Aggregates and big scans favor columnar.

00:03:50.693 --> 00:03:53.936
Columnar plus compression is what lets a system scan petabytes in seconds.

00:03:53.936 --> 00:03:57.179
Next, the canonical columnar system that powers Google.

00:03:57.179 --> 00:04:00.422
BigQuery.
